<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaPairos — Halachos 1 &amp; 2<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE FULL TRANSLATION</span></div>

<div class="sn">Halachah 1 — Sparks in Food; Bless the Favorite First</div>

<div class="p"><i>Halachah:</i> If one has many types of fruit before him, one blesses the most cherished first — except for the Seven Species. See the <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span> for the laws (<span class="h">Siman 211</span>). Also, <span class="h">borai pri ha'aitz</span> takes precedence over <span class="h">borai pri ha'adamah</span>, etc.</div>

<div class="p">Rabbainu <span class="h">nero ya'ir</span> writes (in the <span class="h">Torah</span> <span class="v">"Vayehi Mikaitz — Zikaron,"</span> <span class="lr">Siman 54</span>) that Hashem arranges for every person each day and each hour to elevate sparks according to the portions of <span class="h">nefesh</span>, <span class="h">ruach</span>, and <span class="h">neshamah</span> he has then — see there.</div>

<div class="p">In edible things, holy souls are especially clothed. Hashem arranges for each person to eat the foods connected to his portions of <span class="h">nefesh</span>, <span class="h">ruach</span>, and <span class="h">neshamah</span> at that time, to complete his soul. This is why we bless: <span class="v">"Who creates many souls and their deficiencies [v'chesronam]"</span> — <span class="v">"their deficiencies"</span> specifically — for these souls one now eats are the sparks that were lacking from one's soul and fell there. Now, through eating, one completes one's soul's actual deficiency.</div>

<div class="p">Sometimes a person has no deficiency in his soul, but through eating he elevates souls from there, illuminating and enlivening his soul with additional radiance. This is: <span class="v">"for everything He created to enliven with them the soul of every living being"</span> — as the commentator explains: even without these things one could survive; they are only to enliven further, to add vitality and radiance to one's soul.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one blesses the most cherished first. Every thing draws itself toward its root, where its vitality is connected. Since one cherishes and desires that thing, certainly there are souls there connected to one's current portions of <span class="h">nefesh</span>, <span class="h">ruach</span>, and <span class="h">neshamah</span>. Therefore one blesses it first, for the essential completeness and elevation of the souls is through the <span class="h">berachah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">However, the Seven Species with which <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is praised — there is the root of all holy souls. <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span>, the root of all souls. Therefore <span class="v">"from there comes forth bread"</span> — all holy eating-abundance comes from there, as Rabbainu writes in the <span class="h">ma'amar</span> <span class="v">"Va'achaltem Achol V'Savo'a"</span> (<span class="lr">Siman 47</span>). All foods are the aspect of souls. Therefore the Seven Species have an elevated status and precedence for <span class="h">berachah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is why our Sages established a specific <span class="h">berachah</span> for fruits — <span class="v">"borai pri ha'adamah"</span> or <span class="v">"borai pri ha'aitz"</span> — not including them in the general <span class="v">"shehakol."</span> For in fruits there are very precious souls, as I understood from Rabbainu's conversation. Therefore they are called <span class="h">pri</span> [fruit] — for the importance of souls depends on <span class="h">da'as</span>; the <span class="h">nefesh's</span> essential greatness and nourishment is from <span class="h">da'as</span>, as Rabbainu writes. The closer the <span class="h">nefesh</span> is to <span class="h">da'as</span>, the more important. Therefore they are called <span class="h">pri</span> — for <span class="h">da'as</span> is called <span class="h">pri</span>, since it produces fruit. A specific <span class="h">berachah</span> is designated for them due to this importance, mentioning <span class="h">pri</span> in the blessing. <span class="h">Pri ha'aitz</span> is more important than <span class="h">pri ha'adamah</span> — more precious souls are clothed there, in the aspect of <span class="v">"for man is the tree of the field."</span> Therefore if one blessed <span class="h">pri ha'aitz</span> with <span class="h">borai pri ha'adamah</span>, one has fulfilled the obligation — the advantage of <span class="h">adamah</span> applies to them too. But the reverse does not work. And <span class="h">shehakol</span> covers everything: <span class="v">"that everything came into being through His word"</span> — for souls emerge from speech: <span class="v">"my soul departed when He spoke."</span></div>

<div class="p">Various laws in the <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>: some things growing on trees still receive only <span class="h">borai pri ha'adamah</span> — because the specific <span class="h">berachah</span> for tree-fruit was established only due to their importance. When something is not the essential fruit, it lacks that status. Similarly, unripe fruits receive only <span class="h">ha'adamah</span> — the <span class="h">nefesh</span> there is not yet complete, as Rabbainu writes.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one does not bless on the subordinate [<span class="h">tafel</span>] at all — only on the main item. Everything follows one's intent and <span class="h">da'as</span>. Since this is the main item for him, it is certainly the completeness of his soul. Therefore one blesses it and exempts the <span class="h">tafel</span>, even if it is bread. For although bread is very important and is the aspect of <span class="h">da'as</span>, since at this moment he does not want bread at all but only the main item — the <span class="h">tafel</span> is exempted.</div>

<div class="p">Since the essential completeness and elevation of souls is through <span class="h">da'as</span>, everything follows the person's <span class="h">da'as</span>: if his intent was on certain fruits even though they were not before him, he need not bless again on them — and vice versa. The essential elevation and <span class="h">tikkun</span> depends on the person's <span class="h">da'as</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must treat foods with honor — where souls are clothed, one must treat them with honor so the souls desire to emerge, for their root is <span class="h">kavod</span>. As Rabbainu writes in the <span class="h">ma'amar</span> <span class="v">"Dirshu"</span> (<span class="lr">Siman 37</span>), brought in <span class="h">Hilchos Bais HaKnesses</span> — see there.</div>

<div class="sn">Halachah 2 — <i>Eretz Yisrael</i> Is Made Through <i>Berachah</i>; Seven Species</div>

<div class="p"><i>Topic:</i> <span class="h">Birchos hanehenin</span> and the law of precedence — the Seven Species with which <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is praised take precedence over all; whichever is earlier in the verse precedes in <span class="h">berachah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">We, the holy nation of Israel, must have all our sustenance from the abundance of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. Only <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> belongs to us. The abundance and eating of <span class="h">chutz la'Aretz</span> is unworthy of us — <span class="h">chutz la'Aretz</span> is impure air, under the dominion of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>. Therefore we must rectify all our eating and drinking to be in the aspect of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael's</span> abundance, as though coming from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. This is accomplished through the <span class="h">berachah</span> we recite on everything.</div>

<div class="p">When we bless and thank Hashem for creating them — through this very act, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is made in every place. For the essential holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> was made through <span class="v">"the power of His works He told His nation, to give them the inheritance of the nations"</span> — as Rabbainu writes regarding clapping hands in prayer (<span class="lr">Siman 44</span>). Our Sages: <span class="mx">"Why does Torah begin with Beraishis? Because of 'the power of His works'... by His will He gave it to them, by His will He took it and gave it to us."</span> The essential <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is made by revealing that Hashem created everything and can give it to whomever He wishes. When we bless and thank Him for creating a fruit, we reveal <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness even where that fruit grew.</div>

<div class="p">Our Sages said: <span class="v">"'To Hashem is the earth and its fullness' — before the berachah. 'And the earth He gave to mankind' — after the berachah."</span> After the <span class="h">berachah</span>: <span class="v">"the earth He gave"</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"the power of His works... to give them the inheritance"</span> — through the <span class="h">berachah</span> we acquire the world that Hashem gave us. This is <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> itself. Before the <span class="h">berachah</span>: <span class="v">"to Hashem is the earth and its fullness, the world [taivail]"</span> — Rashi: <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and other lands. After: simply <span class="v">"the earth"</span> — wherever "earth" [<span class="h">eretz</span>] is written without qualification, it is <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, as Rabbainu writes and as is common in our Sages' words. After the <span class="h">berachah</span>, when it is revealed that Hashem created everything and gives it to whom He wills — then <span class="v">"the earth He gave to mankind"</span> — simply "the earth" = <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> — everything becomes <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. Before the <span class="h">berachah</span> there is distinction between <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and other lands. Through the <span class="h">berachah</span>, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is made everywhere.</div>

<div class="p">Also, Rabbainu writes: the essential <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is through Hashem's eyes of providence gazing there constantly: <span class="v">"the eyes of Hashem your G-d are constantly upon it."</span> From this, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness is drawn — through the <span class="h">his'pa'arus</span> Hashem takes in Israel, forming <span class="h">tefillin</span>/<span class="h">mochin</span>, becoming "eyes" through which He gazes — making <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> (see <span class="lr">LM II:40</span>). The <span class="h">berachah</span> accomplishes this: when we bless, praise, and glorify Him, He takes pride in us — <span class="v">"you have declared Hashem, and Hashem has declared you"</span> — we glorify Him, He glorifies us — <span class="v">"Israel, in whom I take pride."</span> Through this, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness is made.</div>

<div class="p">Through the <span class="h">berachah</span>, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is made. Therefore the Seven Species, the praise of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, are themselves <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness, and take precedence in <span class="h">berachah</span>. The essential <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness is through His eyes of providence gazing constantly. The Seven Species contain the Torah's measures — <span class="mx">"the entire verse was said for measures"</span> — Torah measures are the aspect of "eyes," the pupil that contracts vision, the aspect of <span class="mx">"Torah measures"</span> (as explained in <span class="lr">Siman 30</span>). Therefore the Seven Species — Torah measures — are the aspect of the eyes/pupil. Therefore there are seven — corresponding to the seven layers of the eye. Through them <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is praised — its holiness comes from His eyes of providence. Through these seven, one can draw His eyes of providence upon <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. The praise of the Seven Species connects to <span class="v">"the eyes of Hashem are constantly upon it"</span> — His eyes are there through the Seven Species.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore whichever is earlier in the verse precedes in <span class="h">berachah</span> — Torah measures are His eyes of providence, the pupil. What is earlier in Torah is closer to His providence — through which <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness is made, which is the essential <span class="h">berachah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Now the Gemara's question is well resolved: on the verse <span class="v">"a land of wheat and barley"</span> from which precedence is learned — "that verse is needed for its own content, for the whole verse was said for measures!" On the contrary: this is precisely <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael's</span> praise — that these seven are found there, containing Torah measures — the pupil — from which <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness itself is made. For this very reason they should precede in <span class="h">berachah</span> — the essential <span class="h">berachah</span> draws <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> holiness.</div>

<div class="ts">
<div class="sh">✦ Summary — Birchas HaPairos 1 &amp; 2 ✦</div>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>Halachah 1</strong> (<span class="lr">LM I:54</span>): Hashem arranges for each person to eat foods containing sparks of their soul. Bless the favorite first (= closest to your soul). Seven Species from <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> = root of all holy souls → precedence. Specific <em>berachos</em> (<em>ha'aitz</em>, <em>ha'adamah</em>) due to fruits' precious souls. <em>Da'as</em> determines everything — intent, <em>tafel</em>/<em>ikar</em>, precedence.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:.8rem"><strong>Halachah 2</strong> (<span class="lr">LM I:44, LM II:40</span>): Through <em>berachah</em>, <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> is made everywhere. Before <em>berachah</em>: "to Hashem is the earth" (distinction between <em>EY</em> and <em>chutz la'Aretz</em>). After: "the earth He gave to mankind" (<em>eretz</em> unqualified = <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> = everywhere). Seven Species = seven Torah measures = seven layers of the eye = Hashem's eyes of providence on <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. Therefore they precede; earlier in verse = closer to His providence.</p>
</div>

<div class="cn">✦ End of Birchas HaPairos Halachos 1 &amp; 2 — Complete ✦<br><br><i>Na Nach Nachma Nachman May'Uman</i></div>

